@page "/utility-spacing"

<CatHead Title="Spacing utilities">
    Two families, and deliberately only two: the gap inside a row or column, and a top or bottom
    margin on one element. Everything else is a token.
</CatHead>

<div class="cat-note">
    <i class="ri-stack-line"></i>
    <strong>The cascade layer is what makes these work.</strong> A utility is <code>(0,1,0)</code>
    and a component rule like <code>.table th, .table td</code> is <code>(0,1,1)</code>, so a utility
    loses on specificity alone. <code>sedna.utilities</code> sits after <code>sedna.paint</code>, so
    <code>.text-end</code> on a table cell wins regardless of it.
</div>

<div class="cat-note">
    <i class="ri-forbid-line"></i>
    <strong>There is no numeric spacing scale here, and there will not be.</strong> A page assembled
    out of <code>.p-4</code> and <code>.mx-2</code> has moved its design decisions into the markup
    where nobody reviews them. The vertical rhythm between page sections is already the frame's job
    — see <a href="/frame">Shell and nav</a> — and the sizes themselves are
    <a href="/tokens"><code>--space-*</code></a>.
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.UtilitySpacing.Gaps" Title="Gaps" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-row</code> and <code>.sedna-col</code> carry one gap. These four override it:
        <code>.sedna-gap-0</code> removes it, <code>.sedna-gap-1</code> is the default 8px restated,
        and <code>-2</code> and <code>-3</code> are 12px and 20px.
    </p>
    <p>
        Four steps rather than the whole <code>--space-*</code> ramp, because a gap that needs a
        fifth value is usually a layout that wants <a href="/grid">a grid</a>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.UtilitySpacing.Margins" Title="Margins" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-mt-0</code> to <code>-3</code> and <code>.sedna-mb-0</code> to <code>-3</code>,
        on the same 0 / 8 / 12 / 20px steps. The zeros do most of the work: they are the escape from a
        margin a library component sets, without overriding that component's class.
    </p>
    <p>
        Top and bottom only. An inline margin is nearly always the wrong tool — it does not
        collapse, it does not mirror unless it is logical, and the thing being reached for is usually
        a gap or a grid.
    </p>
</CatExample>
